# Farkas–Minkowski Lemma

Let $V$ be a finite-dimensional Euclidean space with inner product $\langle -, - \rangle$. Let $(a_1, \ldots, a_m)$ be a finite family of vectors in $V$, and let $b \in V$.

**The lemma asserts the following equivalence:**

$$
\Big[\;\forall\, x \in V,\ \ \langle a_i, x\rangle \ge 0 \ \text{for all } i=1,\ldots,m \ \Longrightarrow\ \langle b, x\rangle \ge 0\;\Big]
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\Big[\;\exists\, \lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_m \ge 0 \ \text{such that}\ b = \sum_{i=1}^{m} \lambda_i\, a_i\;\Big].
$$

In words:

- **($\Leftarrow$)** If $b$ can be written as a non-negative linear combination $b = \sum_{i=1}^m \lambda_i a_i$ with every $\lambda_i \ge 0$, then for any $x$ satisfying $\langle a_i, x\rangle \ge 0$ for all $i$, we have
$$
\langle b, x\rangle = \sum_{i=1}^m \lambda_i \langle a_i, x\rangle \ge 0,
$$
since it is a sum of non-negative terms. This direction is immediate.

- **($\Rightarrow$)** Conversely, if every $x$ that makes all the inner products $\langle a_i, x\rangle$ non-negative also makes $\langle b, x\rangle$ non-negative, then $b$ must lie in the closed convex cone generated by $\{a_1,\ldots,a_m\}$, i.e., $b$ admits a representation $b = \sum_{i=1}^m \lambda_i a_i$ with all $\lambda_i \ge 0$.

**Geometric reformulation.** Denote by $C = \operatorname{cone}\{a_1,\ldots,a_m\} = \big\{\sum_i \lambda_i a_i : \lambda_i \ge 0\big\}$ the polyhedral cone generated by the $a_i$, and by $C^\circ = \{x \in V : \langle y, x\rangle \ge 0 \text{ for all } y \in C\}$ its polar (dual) cone. The hypothesis on the left-hand side says precisely that $b \in (C^\circ)^\circ$, the bipolar of $C$. Since every polyhedral cone (being closed and convex) coincides with its bipolar, $C = (C^\circ)^\circ$, the conclusion $b \in C$ follows, which is exactly the non-negative representation.

**Remark on the role of finite dimension / closedness.** The cone $C$ generated by finitely many vectors in a finite-dimensional space is closed (it is a polyhedral cone, equivalently a finitely generated cone, hence closed by the finite-generation theorem for cones). The bipolar theorem $C = (C^\circ)^\circ$ holds for closed convex cones containing the origin, so the finite-dimensionality and finite-generation hypotheses are exactly what make the reverse implication work. Without closedness one would only obtain $b \in \overline{C}$ in general.

Thus the Farkas–Minkowski lemma characterizes membership of $b$ in the non-negative span of $\{a_1,\ldots,a_m\}$ by a separation-free, purely "inequality-testing" condition: $b$ is a non-negative combination of the $a_i$ if and only if every vector $x$ that makes non-negative inner product with all the $a_i$ also makes non-negative inner product with $b$.

